From b7c1144e0799ebb837df0a2fa935fbf8586617dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "keepup[bot]" <keepup[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat: cache long router category lists now Decisions is
 GA

The Decisions API went generally available with prompt caching on shared
input. Content in questions is outside the reusable prefix, and the prefix
needs at least 1,024 tokens, so a category list only benefits when it is long
enough and sits in input.

A list at that size now moves to a developer message with an explicit cache
breakpoint, so the user's message is never written to the cache. A cache write
costs 1.25x normal input and lasts at least 30 minutes, so a long list on a
router that gets less than about one message every 30 minutes can pay that
rate on each message instead of getting a discount.

A shorter list keeps its plain choices and adds explicit mode with no
breakpoint, which turns caching off for the request. GA otherwise puts an
implicit breakpoint at the end of input, which would write a long user message
to the cache at the write rate. A router with a short list sees no discount
from this commit.

The size is judged from the list's bytes, at an estimate of about 4 characters
a token, and not by counting tokens. Counting would run a tokenizer over the
list on every classification, and js-tiktoken doesn't know gpt-6-luna, so
TokenManager would fall back to cl100k_base and give an estimate too. A wrong
guess near the minimum only costs a missed discount.

input_json isn't used. The message is plain text, and JSON parts can't carry
a cache breakpoint and are limited to 256 KiB where text parts aren't.

Replay date: 22 Oct 2026, Decisions API generally available.
Source: https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/changelog
